id: rust.tokio-deadline
kind: rust
title: tokio::time deadline (absolute budget)
aliases: deadline, sleep_until, deadline propagation, absolute timeout
tags: async, tokio, timeouts, resilience
questions:
  - how do you bound work by a wall-clock instant instead of a duration?
  - how do you pass a deadline across retries without re-counting?
invariant: A deadline is an absolute instant in time, so it stays correct across retries and restarts; a relative timeout re-counts from wherever it starts.
answer_30s: Use tokio::time::sleep_until or tokio::time::Deadline for an absolute budget, and race it in a tokio::select! so the work is cancelled the moment the instant passes.
when_to_use:
  - cross-retry or cross-restart boundaries where a re-derived timeout would drift
  - any hop that receives a parent's deadline and must honor it
failure_modes:
  - re-deriving a timeout at each retry so the total budget grows without bound
  - dropping the deadline across an await boundary so a child runs longer than the parent
minimal_example: |
  let deadline = tokio::time::Instant::now() + Duration::from_secs(30);
  tokio::select! {
      _ = tokio::time::sleep_until(deadline) => Err(AppError::Timeout),
      r = work() => Ok(r?),
  }
related:
  - rust.timeout
  - design.deadline
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/time/fn.sleep_until.html
